        "markdown": "## Terminal fundamentals\n\n### Explanation\n\nThe terminal accepts written instructions and the shell interprets their syntax. Each session has a working directory. Many commands resolve paths from that directory, so knowing where you are comes before creating, moving or deleting anything. Do not type the prompt symbol shown in a tutorial: a dollar or percent sign usually indicates that the shell is ready.\n\nAn absolute path describes a location from the filesystem root. A relative path starts at the current directory. A dot represents that directory and two dots represent its parent. A folder becomes a useful project location because of how you organize files, not because its name has special powers.\n\nIn PowerShell, Get-Location shows your location, Get-ChildItem lists contents, and Set-Location changes directories. New-Item creates a file or directory according to ItemType. On macOS or Ubuntu in WSL the corresponding tools include pwd, ls, cd, touch and mkdir. Similar aliases do not mean every command or option is interchangeable.\n\nA path error rarely requires reinstalling software. Read the message, check the current directory and spelling, and use Tab completion. Linux paths distinguish uppercase and lowercase. Put a path containing spaces in quotes so the shell treats it as one argument.\n\nUse the command history to revisit a previous instruction, but read it before executing again. A command that was appropriate in one directory may target different files after navigation. Repetition saves typing only when its context still matches your intention.\n\nDeletion commands may bypass the trash. Practice only inside the directory created for this exercise and inspect its contents first. Do not run recursive deletion against your home folder or a repository containing work. A powerful command is not automatically the appropriate response to uncertainty.\n\n### Example\n\nIn native Windows PowerShell:\n\n```powershell\nGet-Location\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory terminal-practice\nSet-Location terminal-practice\nNew-Item -ItemType File notes.txt\nGet-ChildItem\n```\n\nOn macOS or Ubuntu in WSL:\n\n```bash\npwd\nmkdir terminal-practice\ncd terminal-practice\ntouch notes.txt\nls\n```\n\nBoth sequences create a directory, enter it and create an empty file. Do not run both against the same location without checking what already exists. Open notes.txt in the editor, add a sentence and save. The terminal and editor see the same file only when their environment and path agree.\n\n### Guided exercise\n\n1. Choose the appropriate shell and record the starting location. Create the practice directory and verify that it exists before entering it.\n2. Create notes.txt, edit and save it, then list the directory again. Open the file to verify the sentence exists on disk.\n3. Create a subdirectory and enter it. Show the current location, return to the parent and explain which part of the path changed.\n4. Rename only the practice file using Rename-Item in PowerShell or mv in Bash. Verify that its contents remain intact.\n5. Read the help for a command you intend to use. Before removing the practice material, inspect the exact path and contents, delete only the practice file, then remove the empty directory.\n\n### Checkpoint\n\n- Navigate to a directory and back while explaining each path instead of guessing.\n- Identify which commands belong to PowerShell and which belong to Bash.\n- Show what evidence you would inspect before deletion and explain how you recognize a command whose scope is too broad.",
