        "lessonId": "week-03-lesson-03",
        "markdown": "## Arrays and objects\n\n### Explanation\n\nAn array is an ordered collection: its first element has index 0 and its length is the number of elements. An object groups named properties. For a budget, an array represents many entries and each object represents one entry with an identifier, category, and amount in cents. These choices reflect the problem: category is a name, not an array position.\n\nRead an object's property with a dot, as in entry.cents. Read an array element with brackets, as in entries[0]. Reading a nonexistent position returns undefined. An empty array has length zero, so code must not assume entries[0] exists.\n\nStart transformations with a plain loop. An accumulator is a variable that collects a result across iterations. Only after you can trace the loop should you use array methods. filter creates a new array containing elements that pass a condition; map creates one output per input; find returns the first matching element or undefined. reduce combines elements into one result and should receive an initial value so an empty collection has defined behavior.\n\nA callback is a function supplied to another function. The array method calls your callback for each entry. Arrow syntax such as entry => entry.cents is a short function returning that expression. Braces change the form: an arrow body with braces requires an explicit return for a result.\n\nCreating a new array does not automatically clone the objects inside. Filtering entries preserves references to the original entry objects. Mutating a property on a filtered entry can still affect the original. For edits, create a new object with object spread and the changed field, while keeping stable identifiers for later lookup.\n\n### Example\n\n```js\nconst entries = [\n  { id: \"a\", category: \"food\", cents: 8000 },\n  { id: \"b\", category: \"travel\", cents: 4000 },\n  { id: \"c\", category: \"food\", cents: 2500 },\n];\nlet foodTotal = 0;\nfor (const entry of entries) {\n  if (entry.category === \"food\") foodTotal += entry.cents;\n}\nconst food = entries.filter(entry => entry.category === \"food\");\nconst total = food.reduce((sum, entry) => sum + entry.cents, 0);\nconst labels = food.map(entry => entry.id);\nconsole.log(foodTotal, total, labels); // 10500 10500 [\"a\", \"c\"]\n```\nfor...of gives each value, not its index. The reduction starts at zero, then adds 8000 and 2500. A find for an unknown id returns undefined; check for that before accessing its cents property.\n\n### Guided exercise\n\n1. Draw the array and its three objects. Label the difference between index 1, id \"b\", and category \"travel\".\n2. Trace the loop and reduction independently. Confirm they produce the same food total.\n3. Filter a category with no entries. Confirm reducing that result with initial zero returns zero.\n4. Find entry \"b\", then search for \"missing\" and handle the absence with an explicit message.\n5. Build an edited array using map and a new object for entry \"a\". Prove the original cents value is unchanged.\n\n### Checkpoint\n\n- The food total is 10500 cents, the travel total is 4000, and an absent category totals zero.\n- Explain when you would choose map, filter, find, or reduce using your own budget examples.\n- Demonstrate that an immutable edit changes only the new result and does not silently modify the original entries.",
        "wordCount": 454,
